    category: 'INVERSION',
    body: "The adoption of 12-tone equal temperament in the European Baroque (culminating with Bach's Well-Tempered Clavier, 1722) is a cosmological event dressed as a musical convenience. Just intonation — the system it replaced — uses pure integer-ratio intervals derived from the harmonic series (3:2 for the fifth, 5:4 for the major third). These are the same ratios Kepler measured in planetary orbital velocities, the same ratios the Pythagorean tradition identified with cosmic structure. Equal temperament deforms every interval except the octave: the major third is 14 cents sharp, the minor third 16 cents sharp, the fifth 2 cents flat. This is measurable acoustic deformation of cosmologically significant ratios. The inversion: the West gained unlimited transposition and the chromatic richness of tonal harmony while losing the capacity to play in tune with the physical overtone structure of vibrating matter. Two traditions refused this compromise and have maintained it for different reasons: Indian classical music (raga) never adopted equal temperament — all instruments tune to a just drone, and the performance system depends on pure ratios; Harry Partch (1901-1974) independently rebuilt instruments from scratch to escape equal temperament, explicitly framing his work as continuing Pythagoras's project. The two refusals are independent, 2,000 years and an ocean apart, and give the same reason: the pure ratio is not aesthetic preference but cosmological requirement.",
