    body: 'The 110 Hz finding is the most physically specific convergence in the vault\'s acoustic investigation. Three completely independent sacred acoustic traditions target the same narrow frequency band:\n\n**The Maltese Hypogeum (c. -3600 BCE)**: The Oracle Room on the second level resonates at a dominant frequency of approximately 110 Hz, produced by a carved Helmholtz resonator niche tuned to that pitch. Paolo Debertolis and Niccolo Bisconti (University of Trieste, 2014) confirmed the measurement. A male voice chanting in the baritone register — precisely the register a male officiant would use — locks into this frequency and fills the underground space with sustained directionless resonance. The effect is architectural: the sound appears to come from the walls. This is 3,600 years before any surviving text about sacred sound.\n\n**Hindu temple shikharas (c. 600-1300 CE)**: The tower above the garbhagriha (womb chamber) is acoustically engineered to concentrate resonance in the 100-120 Hz range. The vault\'s `music-hindu-temple-shikhara-acoustics` node documents the architectural design: the tower is not structural decoration but a resonating column above the most sacred space. The garbhagriha receives concentrated low-frequency resonance from the tower above. The range is the same.\n\n**Tibetan singing bowls**: Large antique Himalayan singing bowls commonly produce fundamentals in the 110-130 Hz range. The medium singing-bowl range is 220 Hz — exactly one octave above the Hypogeum resonance. Cook et al. (2008) — the same paper that documented the Hypogeum resonance — found that acoustic stimulation in the 110 Hz range shifts regional brain activity from the left prefrontal cortex (analytical processing) toward the right prefrontal cortex (spatial/imaginative processing). This EEG data is from a small sample but has been replicated in subsequent studies.\n\n**What this means**: 110 Hz is the baritone male chanting range — the frequency a male religious specialist naturally produces in a sustained lower-register drone. Three traditions, using different materials and different ritual functions, engineered their sacred acoustic spaces around the frequency of the human male chanting voice. This may not be a mystical convergence but a practical one: if you are optimizing a ritual space for the effect of a male chanting voice, 110 Hz is the acoustic target. The "convergence" might be three independent discoveries of the same acoustic engineering solution to the same design problem.\n\n**The open question**: Is the 110 Hz effect a genuine neurological attractor (this frequency does something specific to the brain that other frequencies do not), or is it simply the natural result of three traditions building for the same human voice range? The EEG data suggests the former. The practical reasoning suggests the latter. Both could be true simultaneously.',
