    id: 'caucasian-alphabet-burst',
    title: 'The Caucasian Alphabet Burst — Highest Density of Script Invention in Any Region, 405–430 CE',
    category: 'ANOMALY',
    summary: 'In a 25-year window (405–430 CE), three fully original writing systems were invented for three neighboring Caucasian nations: Armenian (Mesrop Mashtots, 405 CE), Georgian (c. 430 CE), and Caucasian Albanian (same period). No other region in documented history produced three completely original scripts in a single generation. All three were invented as instruments of religious and political independence simultaneously — Bible translation and sovereignty assertion in a single act. All three were contested or suppressed by dominant powers within years of invention.',
    body: 'The common driver was a geopolitical vice: Caucasian Christian nations (Armenia, Kartli/Georgia, Caucasian Albania) were caught between two imperial powers — Byzantine Greek on the west and Sasanian Persian (Zoroastrian) on the east. Both powers used script as a cultural absorption mechanism: Byzantine Greek spread Christianity and literacy in Greek; Sasanian Persian spread Zoroastrian religious culture in Aramaic/Pahlavi.\n\nMesrop Mashtots (c. 361–440 CE), an Armenian monk, solved this problem by inventing a writing system with no visual connection to either Greek or Syriac — the two obvious model alphabets available. The visual independence of Armenian letterforms was the political argument. Letters that looked like Greek letters could be seen as cultural capitulation to Byzantium. Letters that looked like Syriac could be read as Persian influence. The uniqueness of the Armenian script IS the statement: this is ours, not yours.\n\nThe Armenian Bible translation (completed 434 CE) was called "the Queen of Translations" by early scholars — done directly from Greek and Syriac originals with a team of scholars Mashtots had trained specifically for the task. It produced a literary standard that anchored Armenian identity for sixteen centuries.\n\nGeorgian tradition assigns the script to the same Mashtots (Armenian sources agree); Georgian tradition credits King Pharsman V. Modern scholarship leans toward Mashtots, with Georgian invention following closely from Armenian. The Georgian script similarly avoids visual connection to Greek or Aramaic.\n\nCaucasian Albanian script, for the third Caucasian Christian people, followed the same pattern. It survived only in a handful of inscriptions and a 5th-century manuscript discovered in the 20th century.\n\nThe Armenian genocide (1915–1923) targeted the script specifically: Ottoman policy systematically destroyed Armenian-language schools, presses, and clergy (the literacy-transmission system) as the first organizational phase of extermination. The Armenian script survived in diaspora — in the communities that escaped. It is the best-documented modern case of script-as-resistance, and of deliberate script-destruction as a tool of cultural annihilation.',
